<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xiao Kui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">542–585</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Ming of Western Liang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xiao Kui (542–585) was the second emperor of the Western Liang, a small state around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangling">Jiangling</a> that existed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a>. A respected scholar and writer, he reigned from 562 until his death. His daughter married Yang <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a>, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang of Sui</a>, and became his empress.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Xiao Kui, courtesy name Renyuan, was born in 542 in the reign of his great-grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liang">Emperor Wu of Liang</a>. His father was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-cha">Xiao Cha</a>, Prince of Yueyang, and his mother was Xiao Cha's concubine Lady Cao. After the rebel general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hou-jing">Hou Jing</a> took the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> in 549, Xiao Cha, fearing his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yuan-of-liang">Xiao Yi</a>, surrendered Yong Province to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a>. When Western Wei forces defeated Xiao Yi, they made Xiao Cha emperor of Western Liang, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> holding little more than the land around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangling">Jiangling</a> under a Western Wei commandant. Xiao Kui became crown prince after his elder brother Xiao Liao died, and succeeded in 562.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Xiao Kui was said to be learned, writing some 14 works on filial piety and on fortunetelling, and also highly superstitious; he was considered a capable administrator who let his war-weary subjects recover. In 567 he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> in aiding Hua Jiao, the Chen governor of Xiang Province, who had defected to them, but their forces were defeated at Dunkou by the Chen generals Chunyu Liang and Wu Mingche. In spring 568 Wu Mingche besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangling">Jiangling</a> and tried to flood it; the city held out for over 100 days until Western Liang generals drove him off.</p><p>In 571, on Hua Jiao's advice, Emperor Wu of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> gave the impoverished state three provinces, Ji, Ping and Ruo. In 577 Xiao Kui met Emperor Wu at Yecheng and won his favour by recalling, in tears, what his father had owed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a>, and by comparing him to the mythical emperors Yao and Shun. In 580, when the general Yuchi Jiong rose against the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-sui">Yang Jian</a>, Xiao Kui took the advice of his official Liu Zhuang rather than most of his generals and sided with Yang.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-sui">Yang Jian</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> in 581 as Emperor Wen. In 582 he asked for one of Xiao Kui's daughters as wife for his son Yang Guang, Prince of Jin; the diviners passed over the others and accepted a daughter born in the second month, thought an ill omen, who had been raised by her maternal uncle Zhang Ke. Emperor Wen then withdrew the Commandant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangling">Jiangling</a>. Xiao Kui paid homage at the new capital Daxing in spring 584 and died in summer 585; his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-cong">Xiao Cong</a> succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xiao Kui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Xiao Kui</b>, the second emperor, was a respected scholar, and his daughter married Yang Guang, the future Emperor Yang of the Sui Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-liang-555-587">Western Liang (555-587)</a></li></ul></section>
